1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He studied in Rome. After his father's death, he joined the service in the army of Ferdinand III and as a commander of the division of cuirassiers participated in the Battle of Diedenhoden (Thionville) in the army 
2 CONC of Prince Piccolomini. In 1647 he became commander of the infantry regiment and fought at the Battle of Jihlava, where he was seriously wounded. Later, he participated in the conquests of Krems, Kornneuburg and Opava. That same ye
2 CONC ar he was proposed the commander of Regensburg. In 1648 he happened to stay in the Old Town of Prague, when the Swedes seized the Castle and the Lesser Town. He took part in the defence of the town and in the re-conquest of the Ca
2 CONC stle. The Commander-in-Chief in Prague, the Field Marshal Count Rudolf Colloredo, rewarded Conti for his merits in service and appointed him Imperial Chamberlain and in 1649 Generalwachtmeister. As an excellent engineer he was lat
2 CONC er in charge of building of the fortification system in Prague. In 1651 he resigned from the imperial service and joined Pope Innocent X, after whose death he defended Ferrara against the Duke of Modena. He also accompanied the Sw
2 CONC edish Queen Christina to Rome to Pope Alexander II, who gave him permission to fight for the Republic of Venice against the Turks in Dalmatia. He died after his return to Rome towards the end of 1661.
