1 CONT
1 CONT He was exiled in France.  Buried: Church of the English Benedictines, Paris;
1 CONT later transferred to St. Germain-en-Laye.  Left the country 11 Dec. 1688 and
1 CONT was declared by Parliament (28 Jan 1689) to have abdicated on that day.
1 CONT However,he remained de facto King of Ireland until his defeat at the Battle of
1 CONT the Boyne 1 July 1690.
0 @N6000000006444529447@ NOTE James II of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT James VII and II
1 CONT King of England, King of Scots, and King of Ireland 
1 CONT British Royalty
1 CONT House of Stuart  
1 CONT
1 CONT James VI/I 
1 CONT Children include 
1 CONT    Henry, Prince of Wales 
1 CONT    Elizabeth Stuart 
1 CONT    Charles I 
1 CONT    Robert Stuart, Duke of Kintyre 
1 CONT Charles I 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Charles II 
1 CONT    James VII/II 
1 CONT    Mary, Princess Royal 
1 CONT    Henrietta Anne Stuart 
1 CONT Charles II 
1 CONT James VII/II 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Mary II 
1 CONT    Anne 
1 CONT    James Francis Edward Stuart 
1 CONT Grandchildren 
1 CONT Charles Edward Stuart 
1 CONT Henry Benedict Stuart 
1 CONT Mary II 
1 CONT William III 
1 CONT Anne 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT William, Duke of Gloucester 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT James II of England and VII of Scotland ( 14 October 1633–16 September 1701 ) became King of England, King of Scots, and King of Ireland from 6 February 1685. He was the last Catholic monarch to reign over the Kingdom of England, 
1 CONC Kingdom of Scotland, or Kingdom of Ireland. His subjects distrusted his religious policies and alleged despotism, leading a group of them to depose him in the Glorious Revolution. He was replaced not by his Roman Catholic son, Jam
1 CONC es Francis Edward, but by his Protestant daughter and son-in-law, Mary II and William III, who became joint Sovereigns. James II was the last monarch of Scotland to use the title King of Scots, which had been in use since the firs
1 CONC t monarch of a united Scotland, Kenneth I of Scotland in 843; his successors, Mary II, William III and Anne I used the style "of Scotland" rather than "of Scots".
1 CONT
1 CONT The belief that James—not William III or Mary II—was the legitimate ruler became known as Jacobitism (from Jacobus or Iacobus, Latin for James). James did not himself attempt to return to the Throne, instead living the rest of his
1 CONC  life under the protection of King Louis XIV of France. His son James Francis Edward Stuart and his grandson Charles Edward Stuart (Bonnie Prince Charlie) attempted to restore the Jacobite line after James's death, but failed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Religion 
1 CONT 3 Reign 
1 CONT 4 Glorious Revolution 
1 CONT 5 Later years 
1 CONT 6 Legacy 
1 CONT 7 Style and arms 
1 CONT 8 Issue 
1 CONT 9 Miscellaneous 
1 CONT 10 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT James, the second surviving son of Charles I and Henrietta Maria of France, was born at St. James's Palace in 1633 and created Duke of York in 1644. During the English Civil War—in which his father fought Parliamentary and Puritan
1 CONC  forces—he stayed in Oxford, a Royalist stronghold. When the city surrendered in 1646, the Duke of York was confined in St James's Palace by parliamentary command. In 1648, he escaped from the Palace, whence he went to The Hague i
1 CONC n disguise. When Charles I was executed by the rebels in 1649, monarchists proclaimed the Duke of York's elder brother, Charles, as King Charles II. Charles II was recognised by the Parliaments of Scotland and Ireland, and was cro
1 CONC wned at Scone, in Scotland, in 1651. He was, however, unable to secure the Crown of England, and consequently fled to France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like his brother, the Duke of York sought refuge in France, serving in the French army under Turenne. In 1656, when his brother, Charles, entered into an alliance with Spain—an enemy of France—he joined the Spanish army under Loui
1 CONC s, Prince of Condé. Both Turenne and Condé praised the Duke of York's abilities.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1660, with Oliver Cromwell dead, Charles II was restored to the English Throne, the Duke of York returning to England with him. Though he was the heir-presumptive, it seemed unlikely that the Duke of York would actually inherit
1 CONC  the Crown, for Charles was still a young man capable of fathering children. In September 1660, the Duke of York (who was also created Duke of Albany in Scotland) married the Lady Anne Hyde, the daughter of Charles's chief ministe
1 CONC r, Edward Hyde, 1st Earl of Clarendon.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke of York was appointed Lord High Admiral and commanded the Royal Navy during the Second (1665–1667) and Third Anglo-Dutch War (1672–1674). Following its capture by the English in 1664, the Dutch territory of New Netherland
1 CONC  was named New York in his honour, as was the town of New Amsterdam. Fort Orange, 150 miles up the River Hudson, was renamed Albany in his honour as well. The Duke of York also headed the Royal African Company, which participated 
1 CONC in the slave trade.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Religion
1 CONT The Duke of York was admitted to the Roman Catholic Church in 1668 or 1669. His Protestant enemies in Parliament, led by Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury, ensured the passage of the Test Act; under the Act, all civil
1 CONC  and military officials were required to take an oath (in which they were required not only to disavow the doctrine of transubstantiation, but also denounce certain practices of the Roman Catholic Church as "superstitious and idol
1 CONC atrous") and receive communion under the auspices of the Church of England. The Duke of York refused to perform both actions, instead choosing to relinquish the post of Lord High Admiral.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles II opposed the conversion, ordering that the Duke of York's children be raised as Protestants. Nevertheless, in 1673, he allowed York (whose first wife had died in 1671) to marry the Catholic Mary of Modena. The English pe
1 CONC ople distrusted Catholicism and regarded the new Duchess of York as an agent of the Pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1677, the Duke of York attempted to appease Protestants by allowing his daughter, Mary, to marry the Protestant Prince of Orange, William III (who was also his nephew). Despite the concession, fears of a Catholic monarch persis
1 CONC ted, intensified by the failed pregnancies of Charles II's wife, Catherine of Braganza. A defrocked Anglican clergyman, Titus Oates, falsely spoke of a "Popish Plot" to kill Charles and put the Duke of York on the Throne. The fabr
1 CONC icated plot caused a wave of anti-Catholic hysteria to sweep across the nation. The Duke of York wisely decided to leave England for Brussels. In 1680, the Duke of York was appointed Lord High Commissioner of Scotland and took up 
1 CONC his residence at the Palace of Holyroodhouse in Edinburgh.
1 CONT
1 CONT In England, attempts were made by Lord Shaftesbury and others to exclude the Duke of York from the line of succession. Some even proposed that the Crown go to Charles II's illegitimate son, James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth. When,
1 CONC  in 1679, the Exclusion Bill was in danger of passing, Charles II dissolved Parliament. (The Exclusion Bill crisis contributed to the development of the English two-party system; the Whigs were those who supported the Bill, whilst
1 CONC  the Tories were those who opposed it.) Two further Parliaments were elected in 1680 and 1681, but were dissolved for the same reason.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the dissolution of the Parliament of 1681, no further Parliaments were called. Charles, whose popularity was very high at the time, allowed the Duke of York to return to England in 1682. The Rye House Plot of 1683, a Protest
1 CONC ant conspiracy to assassinate both Charles and the Duke of York, failed utterly; it increased popular sympathy for the King and his brother. York once again found himself influential in government, becoming the leader of the Tory 
1 CONC Party; his brother restored him to the office of Lord High Admiral in 1684.(disputed — see talk page)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT Monarchical Styles of
1 CONT King James II of England 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reference style: His Majesty 
1 CONT Spoken style: Your Majesty 
1 CONT Alternative style: Sir 
1 CONT
1 CONT Monarchical Styles of
1 CONT King James VII of Scotland 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reference style: His Grace 
1 CONT Spoken style: Your Grace 
1 CONT Alternative style: Sir 
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles died sine prole legitima (without legitimate offspring) in 1685, converting to Roman Catholicism on his deathbed. He was succeeded by his brother, who reigned in England and Ireland as James II, and in Scotland as James VI
1 CONC I. James was crowned at Westminster Abbey on 23 April 1685. At first, there was little overt opposition to the new Sovereign; many conservative Anglicans even supported him. The new Parliament which assembled in May 1685 seemed fa
1 CONC vourable to James, agreeing to grant him a large income.
1 CONT
1 CONT James, however, faced the Monmouth Rebellion (led by Charles II's illegitimate son, the Duke of Monmouth). James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth declared himself King on 20 June 1685, but was afterwards defeated at the Battle of Sedge
1 CONC moor. Monmouth was executed at the Tower of London soon afterwards. Despite the lack of popular support for Monmouth, James began to distrust his subjects.
1 CONT
1 CONT His judges—most notably, George Jeffreys, 1st Baron Jeffreys (the "Hanging Judge")—punished the rebels brutally. Judge Jeffreys' Bloody Assizes led the public to see their King as a cruel and barbarous ruler. To protect himself fr
1 CONC om further rebellions, James sought to establish a large standing army. By putting Roman Catholics in charge of several regiments, the King was drawn into a conflict with Parliament. Parliament was prorogued in November 1685, neve
1 CONC r to meet again during James's reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Religious tension intensified in 1686. In the collusive case of Godden v. Hales, a panel of judges of the Court of King's Bench were coerced by the King into declaring that the King could dispense with the religious restrictions i
1 CONC mposed by the Test Acts. Taking advantage of the dispensing power, James controversially allowed Roman Catholics to occupy the highest offices of the Kingdom. He received at his court the papal nuncio, Ferdinando d'Adda, the first
1 CONC  representative from Rome to London since the reign of Mary I. James's Jesuit confessor, Edward Petre, was a particular object of Protestant ire. These policies caused the King to lose the support of his former allies, the Tories.
1 CONT
1 CONT James then ordered the suspension of Henry Compton, the anti-Catholic Bishop of London; several other Anglicans in political office were dismissed. In the Declaration of Indulgence (1687), he suspended laws punishing Roman Catholi
1 CONC cs and other religious dissenters. (It is unclear if James issued the Declaration to gain the political support of the dissenters, or if he was truly committed to the principle of freedom of religion). James also dissolved Parliam
1 CONC ent in 1687, afterwards reforming the government so as to reduce the power of the nobility.
1 CONT
1 CONT The King also provoked opposition by his policies relating to the University of Oxford. He offended Anglicans by allowing Catholics to hold important positions in Christ Church and University College, two of Oxford's largest colle
1 CONC ges. Even more unpopularly, he dismissed the Protestant Fellows of Magdalen College, appointing a wholly Roman Catholic board in their place. Controversially, James accredited the Papal Nuncio and granted public offices to four Ca
1 CONC tholic bishops.
1 CONT
1 CONT James granted three Londoners and Virginia Catholic George Brent rights of religious freedom for the settlement of French Huguenots on the 30,000 acre (121 km²) Brenttown (Brenton) tract in old Prince William County, Virginia in 1
1 CONC 687. Richard Foote, nephew of Nicholas Hayward (one of the founding partners), settled at Chotank in King George County, Virginia to manage the project. Nicholas Hayward marketed Brenttown to English Catholics after the Glorious R
1 CONC evolution eliminated most political reasons for French Protestants to leave England.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Glorious Revolution
1 CONT
1 CONT Half-Crown coin of James II, 1686. The inscription reads IACOBUS II DEI GRATIA (James II by the Grace of God) 
